WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.314 --> 00:00:02.630
(creepy music)

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:04.770
- Horror films don't get much respect.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.000
But while horror has often
been dismissed as lowbrow,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.667
its music has been in-cra-ba.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.240
- Listen to the score from the 2018

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.104
horror thriller Bird Box.

00:00:15.104 --> 00:00:17.650
(tense music)

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.142
Now check this out.

00:00:19.142 --> 00:00:21.300
("Polymorphia" by Krzysztof Penderecki)

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.600
Hear the similarities?

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.180
This experimental
composition was a key part

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.160
of The Exorcist, a film that
changed horror music forever.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.840
But what is that sound and
how did it come to influence

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.553
the horror films of today?

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.840
- Nahre and I are gonna compose a score

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.860
for an original scary short.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.260
But first, let's take a
look back at how scary music

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.330
has evolved from the silent
era to The Exorcist to today.

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:49.470
- Since there's been
film, there's been horror.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:53.140
In the silent era, most films
would either have no music

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.410
or they would be accompanied
by live musicians

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.370
who would play popular or classical tunes.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.310
Only a handful of silent horror films

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.470
were accompanied by original scores.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.440
The music written for the 1920 release

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.500
of The Cabinet of Dr.
Caligari was described

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.687
by one theater runner as
"eligible for citizenship

00:01:10.687 --> 00:01:12.227
"in a nightmare country".

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.850
The first time a thematic
musical score was written

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.060
for a feature-length American talkie

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.233
was for the 1933 monster movie King Kong.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.570
Written by composer Max Steiner,

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.740
this score utilized
techniques that would go on

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.920
to be used in horror films for decades,

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.440
such as Mickey Mousing, in which music

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.320
matches a character's action

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.450
and leitmotifs which are
short recurring musical ideas

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.370
that typically signify the presence

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:45.000
of a monster or a villain.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.000
It's a technique John
Williams would later use

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.960
in what's likely one of the most memorable

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.686
film scores of all time.

00:01:50.686 --> 00:01:53.603
(Jaws theme plays)

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.040
This two note leitmotif
is perfect not only

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:01.230
for its menacing simplicity
but also for its functionality.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.412
The quicker it gets, the
closer we know the shark is.

00:02:04.412 --> 00:02:06.510
(woman screams)

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.930
- During Hollywood's
golden age, horror films

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.810
and the scores written for
them often lacked subtlety.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.150
The music usually told
the audience what to fear.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.080
- 50s was so big, giant
ants and these aliens

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.380
from outer space, where
60s, Psycho kicked it off.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.430
It was personal.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.250
It's a lot of interpersonal drama.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:28.944
Bernard Herrmann's
score in that is iconic.

00:02:28.944 --> 00:02:32.530
(tense music)
(she screams)

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.900
- Psycho's shower scene is
one of the earliest examples

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.690
of the jump scare and is one
of the most famous examples

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.430
of a stinger chord, a common
horror music technique

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.270
where musicians sharply attack the chord

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.861
to reinforce the moment of surprise.

00:02:44.861 --> 00:02:46.460
(suspenseful music)

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:49.610
Hitchcock originally wanted
the shower scene to be silent,

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:52.260
but composer Bernard Herrmann
decided to write a piece

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.600
featuring violins playing the
shrieking upward glissando,

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.890
which is produced when a
violinist glides their fingers

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.690
up or down the strings as they play.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.057
Hitchcock later said that
"33% of the effect of Psycho

00:03:05.057 --> 00:03:06.350
"was due to the music".

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:07.610
- I watch a lot of superhero movies.

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:09.630
Sure, the Avengers score is cool,

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.590
but I'm not gonna think
of the Avengers score

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.310
when I'm kicking butt.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:14.750
But when I'm scared alone at night,

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.370
I think of that horror movie score.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:17.610
- One reason the stinger chord

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.087
has so effectively heightened
terror in horror films

00:03:20.087 --> 00:03:22.720
is that they manipulate the
deep-rooted fear centers

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:23.920
in our brains.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.130
- What makes something scary?

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.150
Experimental work has
shown is that screams

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.170
that are fear-based screams,
they're known to have

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.600
a very particular kind
of acoustic signature.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:32.710
It's called roughness.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:36.100
And that gives it the kind
of scraggly, fluttery vibe,

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.670
whether they're vocalized
sounds or alarm sounds

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.440
or other sounds, it's the ones that have

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.250
increased roughness feature,
and that's what you interpret

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:45.180
as a listener as fearful.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.420
It could be a guitar string,
it could be a piano sound.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:48.253
It could be anything.

00:03:48.253 --> 00:03:49.680
- Now let's fast forward to the film

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.890
that changed horror music
forever, The Exorcist.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:56.420
William Friedkin's 1973
classic was so terrifying

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:59.330
that it caused audience
members to faint, vomit,

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:01.450
and protest its release.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:04.530
Some people even thought the
film itself was possessed.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.720
That's partly because of its
unforgettable soundtrack,

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.720
which included disturbing
experimental compositions

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.140
that went on to shape
the way horror scores

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.124
and soundtracks sound, even to this day.

00:04:17.124 --> 00:04:22.124
("Polymorphia" by Krzysztof Penderecki)

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.430
That's the sound of Krzysztof Penderecki,

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:27.370
the leader of the Polish School
of Avante-Garde Composers

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:29.830
in the 1950s and 60s.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:32.780
To get this haunting sound,
Penderecki used tools

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.750
like sound masses and tone clusters.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:39.010
- Penderecki's score, I don't
know, it sounds classic.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:41.040
I love a song that I don't
know when it was composed,

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.450
I didn't know if Penderecki
was from the 1700s or now

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.780
and I love that it has
this timeless sound to it.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:50.620
It's timelessly scary, as well.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:52.180
- So to create a tone cluster,

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.500
you basically take a number of pitches

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.320
that are a semi-tone apart.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.240
For example, F sharp, G, G sharp.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.740
(plays notes)

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:04.400
These are all tone clusters.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.740
With sound mass, the focus is
more on the effect as a whole,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.583
where we have a clump of pitches

00:05:10.583 --> 00:05:14.040
(plays sound mass)

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.670
where the specificity
of each note and rhythm

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:19.343
matters less than the texture as a whole.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.860
His use of these compositional
tools defy the classical form

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:29.100
and help take The Exorcist
to a demonic dimension.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.920
- Although Penderecki's music
feels like a horror film,

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.630
he didn't write it for the screen.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.740
Much of his experimental work was inspired

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:38.430
by the real-life terrors of World War II.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:41.000
As a boy in Poland, Penderecki
witnessed the violence

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.434
of Nazi's in his hometown of Debica.

00:05:43.434 --> 00:05:45.150
Penderecki's music was also used

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.180
in another landmark horror film,

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.833
Stanley Kubrick's The Shining.

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:52.209
Horror music, horror film.

00:05:52.209 --> 00:05:55.600
- I've never seen a real horror film

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:58.180
from start to finish ever, ever.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:05:59.750
- [LA] Is this not your cup of tea?

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:01.420
- When I was little, I used to get scared

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:03.830
watching documentaries about ...

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.600
You know those documentaries that talk

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.218
about haunted houses or whatever?

00:06:08.218 --> 00:06:11.347
- Yeah, but that stuff be real, though.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.240
- Do you think that
there's a stereotypical

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.330
horror film sound?

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:20.120
- [LA] I think the shrieking violins.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:21.857
- [Nahre] Stuff in the
higher register like ...

00:06:21.857 --> 00:06:23.730
(high notes play)
(he yells)

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:24.960
- [LA] Yeah.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.740
- Or even any dissonant interval.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:31.010
Dissonance is a common
aspect of horror film music.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:33.210
One of the most effective
ways to utilize it

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:36.200
is through the tritone,
musical interval composed

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.530
of three adjacent whole-tones.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.490
A major third.
(plays major third)

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:42.620
Or a perfect fifth.
(plays perfect fifth)

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.700
These are all consonant intervals

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.473
where they sit quite
comfortably in the ear.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:50.995
Let's compare that to a tritone.

00:06:50.995 --> 00:06:52.507
(plays tritone)

00:06:52.507 --> 00:06:53.370
There's more tension there.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.770
It's a clashing, dissonant
sound that has helped it earn

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.220
the name The Devil's Interval in music.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:00.180
And you can hear it in many horror films,

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.180
like during the canoe
scene in Friday the 13th,

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:06.700
where composer Harry
Manfredini repeats two tritones

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:08.083
a half-step apart.

00:07:08.083 --> 00:07:10.583
(tense music)

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:14.450
- Another common technique
is to put familiar

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:17.420
or non-threatening music
into a creepy context.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:20.391
Often, it's little kids
singing children's song.

00:07:20.391 --> 00:07:23.141
(haunting music)

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:27.340
Also, quoting religious
music can help set the stage

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:29.370
for films that traffic in the demonic.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:32.220
Wendy Carlos and Rachel
Elkind used this technique

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.777
in the opening of The
Shining, with a recomposition

00:07:34.777 --> 00:07:38.420
of Dies Irae, a 13th
century Gregorian chant.

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:41.330
(sinister music)

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:43.870
- Playing instruments in
specific or extreme ways

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:46.710
is another powerful tool
in horror film music.

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:49.320
You'll often hear violins
play sul ponticello,

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.070
which means close to the bridge,

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:54.853
combined with tremolo to
produce eerie me-shrul sounds.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.080
Sometimes, simple techniques
can be just as effective

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.000
at heightening dread in horror films,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.000
such as using a bassline to
mimic the sound of a heartbeat,

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.600
as John Carpenter did in The Thing.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.230
The steady heartbeat gives us a clue

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:14.510
that something menacing is
constantly lurking nearby

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:16.474
and it's coming for the characters.

00:08:16.474 --> 00:08:19.307
(heartbeat music)

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:23.223
Say that, just with strings,

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.800
there's something about
sustaining a high note.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:28.853
Like, can you hear that?

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:33.920
That just builds tension
the more you do it.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:35.290
- True.
- Also really

00:08:35.290 --> 00:08:36.843
low things like ...

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:40.493
- Yeah.
- And then you put a cluster.

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:48.820
- Do you want to approach
the me riding the bike

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:50.410
in the hallway in front of the twins?

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:52.750
I'll approach the door.
- Okay.

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:54.990
- Now here's a score that
we made for a horror film

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:58.250
starring Too-sant Morrison
from our fellow PBS series,

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:00.404
America From Scratch.

00:09:00.404 --> 00:09:02.904
(eerie music)

00:09:11.073 --> 00:09:13.323
- Little pigs, little pigs,

00:09:14.866 --> 00:09:16.116
let me come in.

00:09:18.281 --> 00:09:21.531
Not by the hair of my chinny-chin-chin.

00:09:22.996 --> 00:09:27.996
And I'll huff, and I'll puff,
and I'll blow your house in.

00:09:28.642 --> 00:09:30.892
(ax thuds)

00:09:38.484 --> 00:09:40.984
(eerie music)

00:09:59.103 --> 00:10:00.103
- [Both] Hi.

00:10:03.149 --> 00:10:04.982
Come and play with us.

00:10:07.055 --> 00:10:08.888
Come and play with us.

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:13.850
- Monstrum is a new show
from PBS Digital Studios

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:16.150
about monsters, myth, and lore.

00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:19.220
This show is written and
